{id:"d1",section:"S2",cloud:"Touch UI dialogs (Granite UI) + Core Components under /libs/core/wcm/components, resolved via sling:resourceType/SuperType — /apps checked before /libs.",legacy:"Classic UI dialogs used xtype-based widget definitions; foundation components lived under different /libs paths than today's Core Components.",trap:"See xtype or /libs/foundation/components in a question? That's describing the legacy model — don't map it onto AEMaaCS behavior."},
{id:"d2",section:"S1",cloud:"AEMaaCS: no direct filesystem/network access to the dispatcher. Invalidation goes through a Dispatcher Flush replication agent, triggered via the Replication API.",legacy:"On-prem: dispatcher ran on your own Apache server with direct filesystem access to the cache docroot — cache files could be deleted by hand.",trap:"An option describing ‘delete the cache files directly’ or ‘restart dispatcher to clear cache’ is the legacy/on-prem answer — on AEMaaCS it's always Replication API → Flush agent."},
{id:"d3",section:"S1",cloud:"AEMaaCS restricts direct Felix console config editing on higher environments — configs are deployed as code (ui.config package content), often paired with repoinit for service users.",legacy:"Configs could be clicked directly into /system/console/configMgr, including on something close to production.",trap:"‘Just add the OSGi config via the Felix console’ is a legacy-instinct trap on a higher-environment question — the cloud-correct path is code + repoinit, not console clicks."},
{id:"d4",section:"S1",cloud:"Touch UI: Component Policies + the Style System — console-configured, template/policy-driven, no Classic UI equivalent.",legacy:"cq:editConfig + xtype-based dialogs configured what could be edited and where a component could be placed.",trap:"If a question shows cq:editConfig alongside xtype dialogs, that's the legacy configuration model, not how Policies/the Style System work today."},
{id:"d5",section:"S3",cloud:"AEMaaCS enforces /apps and /libs as genuinely immutable at the platform level — no prod hand-edits; everything ships through code + the Cloud Manager pipeline.",legacy:"On-prem allowed hand-editing config or content directly on the server.",trap:"Any answer suggesting a direct/manual edit to a running AEMaaCS environment is wrong by construction — the cloud-correct path always routes through Cloud Manager."},
{id:"d6",section:"S4",cloud:"AEMaaCS surfaces most operational visibility through Cloud Manager instead — there's no full local equivalent.",legacy:"6.5 on-prem exposed the Operations Dashboard and hand-configured replication agents visible on the local server.",trap:"A question describing the ‘Operations Dashboard’ or manually configuring a replication agent on the instance is describing the on-prem/AMS model — recognize it as legacy, don't go looking for it in AEMaaCS."},
{id:"d7",section:"S2",cloud:"Universal Editor is Adobe's current recommendation for new projects.",legacy:"SPA Editor is now marked deprecated for new projects (existing SPA Editor projects are still supported).",trap:"Know the SPA Editor mechanism for the exam — it may still be tested — but don't treat it as current best-practice guidance for a new build."},
{id:"d8",section:"S1",cloud:"AEMaaCS fronts every environment with a managed CDN sitting in front of the Dispatcher — Dispatcher is still author-configured (dispatcher.any/filter.any) but is now one layer in a stack: CDN (closest to the user) → Dispatcher → AEM.",legacy:"On-prem/AMS, the Dispatcher (paired with your own web server) was typically the outermost cache layer devs reasoned about directly, with no managed CDN necessarily in front of it.",trap:"A question that treats the Dispatcher as the only/outermost cache layer is using the on-prem mental model — on AEMaaCS, always account for the CDN sitting in front of it too. (Verified against Adobe's official Dispatcher documentation.)"}
